  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The risk of severe thunderstorms seems to be\ndecreasing through tonight, although upstream thunderstorm \ncomplexes still need to be monitored.\n\nThe thunderstorm complex that was in Ohio earlier dove southward\ntoward higher instability in far southwest VA, with only some\nstratified rain affecting western/southwestern portions of the\nCWA over the next couple hours. Additional thunderstorms across\nwestern Ohio, Indiana, and Illinois are now also showing signs\nof diving southward due to the cold pool left by the eastern\nOhio storms. While some 12Z CAMs showed remnants of this\nconvection reaching the area this evening, this is increasingly\nin question. East of the dense overcast, there has been some\nfiltered heating is occurring. This will result in some\ninstability, but it isn't clear there will be enough of a\ntrigger to kick off any storms locally. Most recent CAMS have\nbacked off on local development, but can't totally rule out a\nfew isolated to scattered storms mainly east of the Blue Ridge.\nShear is a bit more marginal today, but it's still possible\nthere could be a stronger storm with gusty to damaging winds.\nThis threat would obviously be highest if an organized complex\nmoved in from the west.\n\nGiven the current trajectories of the storms to the west, it's\npossible additional heavy rain avoids Garrett County. If these\ntrends continue, the Flood Watch may be cancelled early.\n\nBased on current models, the second half of the night should\nlargely be dry. There may be some patchy fog if clearing occurs.\nLows will be in the 60s to lower 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms remain possible at\ntimes Wednesday through Friday.\n\nThe west to east oriented cold front will remain near the area\nthrough Friday. The main question is whether we remain in west\nor northwest flow and more stable conditions, or if the richer\nmoisture is able to advect back in from the southwest. Periods\nof cloud cover could also be an issue that affects diurnal\ndestabilization. Many, but not all, models show a relative lull\non Wednesday as the greatest instability remains south and west\nof the area.\n\nOne, possibly two, waves of low pressure may track along the\nfront between Wednesday night and Friday. This could lift the\nfront northward, or at least provide a more notable source of\nforcing other than subtle shortwaves aloft. It's unclear how\nwell shear, instability, and forcing will overlap, but it is\npossible some stronger storms could develop if they do. ML/AI\nguidance has mixed signals on severe probabilities, but overall\nlower compared to Monday and today.\n\nTemperatures will likely be within several degrees of normal\nthrough Friday with elevated humidity levels.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Drier conditions anticipated heading into next \nweek, with a few showers & thunderstorms possible late this \nweekend. \n\nDry air moves in from the Great Lakes region at the start of the \nweekend as upper-level ridging builds over the lower Mississippi \nValley by Saturday. Temperatures remain slightly cooler than average \nduring this period with fewer precipitation chances overall. As \nthis high pressure builds towards the south, shortwave energy \nforming north of the ridge over the Great Lakes will track towards \nthe Mid-Atlantic Sunday and heading into Monday. This could produce \nchances for additional showers and thunderstorms, with most medium-\nrange severe outlooks currently producing rather marginal 5-15\npercent probabilities for severe thunderstorms. \n\nOnce this shortwave system passes through, zonal flow transitions \nover the region, reducing thunderstorm chances overall. Temperatures \nwill start to increase during this period as surface high pressure \ntransitions into the region.",
